// WHY: the executor `Token` — the target-token type the generated parser and
// the grammar executor match terminals against. The tokens themselves are now
// produced by the SHIPPING streaming lexer via `lex_real` (real_lex.av), which
// bridges each `Tk` to this shape; the old demo tokeniser `lex_expr` that this
// file used to carry is retired (it double-lexed and produced only the string
// projection, no `Tk`). See real_lex.av for the production producer.
//
// Token kinds: NUMBER STRING BOOL IDENT PUNCT EOF (the `kind` string, a lossy
// projection of `tk` via tk_to_grammar_kind). A named terminal in the grammar
// (`NUMBER`) matches by `kind`; a literal terminal (`"+"`, `"true"`) matches by
// `tk` (fast enum compare) when the emitter can name the variant, else `text`.

use core.{Tk}
use features.grammar.{GKind}

/// One target token. `pos` is the byte offset of the token start (used for
/// span-ish diagnostics). `tk` is the parser's Tk-enum kind (ps3t.6.7 perf:
/// lex_real stores it verbatim from `current_kind`, so the generated parser
/// dispatches on the enum — `st.cur().tk == Tk.X` — instead of string-comparing
/// `text`, eliminating the avra_streq cost the string-keyed dispatch paid per
/// descent level). The string `kind` (tk_to_grammar_kind's projection) stays for
/// the named-terminal executor path (`NUMBER`/`STRING`/`IDENT`/…).
export type Token = { kind: GKind, text: string, pos: int, tk: Tk }
